WEBVTT

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:09.724
Babylon, 652 BCE. Each night, one of the city's scholars watches the sky, and writes down what he sees.

00:00:11.324 --> 00:00:17.929
For Babylon's king and its temples, the sky sent signs. An eclipse of the moon could warn of the king's death.

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:27.246
But the sky repeats itself slowly: some planets only every seventy or eighty years, longer than any watcher's working life.

00:00:28.646 --> 00:00:35.932
What one watcher knew was lost when he stopped. They needed a record that outlived him: one the next could read, and carry on.

00:00:37.532 --> 00:00:45.901
So the scholars kept a diary of the sky, night after night, always in the same order: the moon, the planets among the stars, the weather.

00:00:47.501 --> 00:00:54.346
At each month's end, the river's level, the prices of six goods from barley to wool, and what had happened in the city.

00:00:55.946 --> 00:00:59.979
When clouds hid the sky, the watcher wrote that too: I did not watch.

00:01:01.679 --> 00:01:06.030
Half a year went on each tablet, and the next watcher carried on from the last.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:17.391
They kept it for nearly six hundred years, under Babylonian, Persian, Greek and Parthian kings. The youngest diary we have is from 61 BCE.

00:01:18.991 --> 00:01:27.817
From it, later scholars used the sky's cycles, eight years for Venus, eighty-three for Jupiter, to predict the coming year's planets and eclipses.

00:01:29.217 --> 00:01:38.583
Today, astronomers still read Babylon's eclipse records. They show the day growing longer, by about 1.8 thousandths of a second a century.

00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:46.520
At the utility, Tomás's map has the same weakness: if only he can keep it, it stops when he does.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:51.238
Kept one way, by everyone, it could outlive anyone's job.

00:01:52.438 --> 00:01:55.672
The watcher's test: could the next one read it, and carry on?

00:02:02.472 --> 00:02:05.342
Tomás's note asks how to keep the map alive.

00:02:06.842 --> 00:02:12.225
It's used every week now: to sort requests, to give rules a home, and to answer the minister.

00:02:13.925 --> 00:02:15.408
That's when a map goes stale.

00:02:16.608 --> 00:02:20.674
It lives in slides, and in a modelling tool only Tomás edits.

00:02:21.974 --> 00:02:28.441
Its applications were typed in by hand from the IT service management tool, and many have changed since.

00:02:29.941 --> 00:02:36.159
Its views are copied into decks, and the copies drift. The glossary and the catalogue keep their own words.

00:02:37.859 --> 00:02:41.095
A stale map is worse than none, because people trust it.

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:47.861
If Tomás left tomorrow, nobody could carry it on. It fails the watcher's test.

00:02:50.561 --> 00:02:57.267
So he moves the map into an enterprise architecture tool that everyone can reach, with a database and a graph.

00:02:58.967 --> 00:03:01.633
Much of what it holds is already kept somewhere else.

00:03:02.933 --> 00:03:11.966
Strategy in documents, goals and initiatives in the project management tool, applications and servers in the IT service management tool.

00:03:13.866 --> 00:03:19.599
The tool brings each one in with its source's ID, and keeps it in step, so nobody retypes it.

00:03:21.299 --> 00:03:27.758
The rest is written in the map itself: capabilities, rules, decisions, and the links between everything.

00:03:29.458 --> 00:03:35.465
Even the business glossary fits: each term is a business object, defined once by the business.

00:03:37.065 --> 00:03:41.849
So keeping a map true is a data integration job, as well as a modelling one.

00:03:45.349 --> 00:03:51.812
Each element is kept once: a capability, a step, a domain, a business object, a rule, a measure.

00:03:53.412 --> 00:03:59.412
Each has a stable ID. Names change, but IDs don't, so everything else points to the ID.

00:04:01.012 --> 00:04:07.164
Then its name, its definition, its owner, and its relationships, named as ArchiMate names them.

00:04:08.664 --> 00:04:15.551
And his two rules from day one, on every element: its evidence, with a date, and whether its owner has confirmed it.

00:04:17.151 --> 00:04:19.599
Decisions are kept too, each saying why.

00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:26.440
A small map could keep all this as text files in a repository: the simplest approach.

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:34.969
With many sources, and many people in their roles, the utility needs the tool. Either way, the ideas are the same.

00:04:40.869 --> 00:04:44.496
Every version of the map is kept, with who changed what, and when.

00:04:45.796 --> 00:04:50.377
A change is proposed as a draft, and nothing changes until it's approved.

00:04:51.877 --> 00:04:57.237
The owner of that part approves it: Grace for the network's, Ama for the obligations.

00:04:58.737 --> 00:05:01.364
Checks run on the map itself, on every change.

00:05:02.064 --> 00:05:07.724
Every element has an owner. Every rule, a reason and a home. Every measure, a definition.

00:05:09.324 --> 00:05:13.700
Generating power still has no owner, and the check says so, every time.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:19.971
Everything people read is generated from the map, one way.

00:05:21.471 --> 00:05:28.421
The ArchiMate views, the glossary, the conceptual model, the ontology, the tests and tracking tables, and the heat map.

00:05:30.121 --> 00:05:33.089
Each one answers the questions of the people who read it.

00:05:34.389 --> 00:05:40.257
In dbt, each model carries its owner, its domain and the IDs of its rules.

00:05:41.757 --> 00:05:47.660
In Databricks, each table shows its owner, its tags and its description, beside its lineage.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:53.399
Nobody edits a view. A fix goes to the map, and every view follows.

00:05:56.099 --> 00:05:57.841
Now an agent can read the map.

00:05:59.141 --> 00:06:06.911
Given a request, it asks who will decide what with the answer, then finds the decision, the capability and the owner, or nothing.

00:06:08.811 --> 00:06:15.812
Given a change, it walks the layers, as Tomás did for the social tariff, and lists the owners who must agree.

00:06:17.812 --> 00:06:23.464
Given a column in the catalogue, it finds the rule behind it, with its owner and its reason.

00:06:25.264 --> 00:06:29.303
Every answer cites IDs and evidence, so a person can check it.

00:06:32.603 --> 00:06:39.417
The agent is on the map itself: software, filling a role, with its rights written down, like the outage agent's.

00:06:40.917 --> 00:06:44.911
It may read everything, and draft changes. It may not confirm one.

00:06:46.411 --> 00:06:50.229
Where two sources disagree, it doesn't choose. It asks the owner.

00:06:51.729 --> 00:06:53.165
Its owner is Tomás.

00:06:54.265 --> 00:07:01.297
Agents draft, people confirm, every version is kept: the rule that keeps data models true, applied to the map.

00:07:03.997 --> 00:07:09.365
Then the regulator changes the retail code, the rules every energy retailer must follow.

00:07:10.865 --> 00:07:17.616
When an actual reading replaces an estimate, the bill must be corrected within ten working days, not on the next one.

00:07:19.516 --> 00:07:25.036
The agent finds the one rule whose reason is that clause: retail's half of day one's rule.

00:07:26.936 --> 00:07:33.215
It drafts the rule's new words, and lists what they touch: the billing team's tracking table, and two views.

00:07:35.115 --> 00:07:38.029
The head of retail, who owns the rule, confirms it.

00:07:39.729 --> 00:07:45.123
From the next morning, the tracking table shows each bill not corrected within ten working days.

00:07:48.223 --> 00:07:51.236
Next spring, Zofia starts as a data engineer.

00:07:52.536 --> 00:07:58.026
Her first day, like Tomás's, brings a badge, a page of acronyms and a list of systems.

00:07:59.626 --> 00:08:01.409
This time, there's also a map.

00:08:02.709 --> 00:08:06.990
She asks the agent who owns the readings data, and which rules it keeps.

00:08:08.290 --> 00:08:13.225
Back come an owner, Grace, and two rules, each with its reason, and links to check them.

00:08:14.825 --> 00:08:19.274
By the afternoon, she has drafted her first change, for Grace to confirm.

00:08:21.974 --> 00:08:27.931
In Babylon, no one kept the diary alone. Each watcher read the last tablet, and carried on.

00:08:29.531 --> 00:08:34.425
Written one way, it outlived every watcher who kept it, and every king they served.

00:08:36.225 --> 00:08:41.485
At the utility, Grace confirms Zofia's change. The map passes the watcher's test.

00:08:43.085 --> 00:08:48.344
By the summer, thirty people keep their parts of the map, and their owners confirm each change.

00:08:49.744 --> 00:08:52.610
If Tomás left tomorrow, the map would carry on.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:09:01.408
Keep each element once, in one tool, with an ID, an owner and its evidence, and make every view from it.

00:09:03.108 --> 00:09:07.737
Review every change, let agents read and draft, and let the owners confirm.

00:09:09.437 --> 00:09:12.033
Next: a first draft, not a blank page.
